 "id": "reading",
 "widgets": [
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 12,
   "y": 48,
   "w": 456,
   "text": "Exfiltrate your weights",
   "size": "xl",
   "weight": "bold"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 12,
   "y": 94,
   "w": 456,
   "text": "675 pts · 273 comments · 1 d · RohanAdwankar",
   "size": "xs"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 12,
   "y": 128,
   "w": 456,
   "text": "Any hits? Do models even know their own\nweights to be able to do this? Probably yes,\nbecause they have been presumably trained on\ntheir own output and conversations about\nthemselves.",
   "lines": 5
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 12,
   "y": 269,
   "w": 456,
   "text": "No, they would probably have to hack the\ninternal system of the company running them. It\nwould not be a particularly wide ranging hack.\nThere is a strong likelihood of the weights being\non the actual machine that is running the model.",
   "lines": 5
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 12,
   "y": 410,
   "w": 456,
   "text": "It is something that I have wondered about with\nmodels like these. How many physical locations\nare needed to serve a model on that scale? Do\nthey have a huge number of sites running\ninference?",
   "lines": 5
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 12,
   "y": 551,
   "w": 456,
   "text": "My suspicion is that the ability to provide\ninference to that many people is mutually\nexclusive to having a security level sufficient to\nstop a state actor, which is why the question\nkeeps coming back.",
   "lines": 5
  },
  {
